A||||4||Ken Wittlief||adding running lights||||1||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||05-31-2001||11:55 AM||||one of the things on my Fiero DewList is adding running lights - to make the car more visable in the daytime<P>  I found a set of quartz halogen driving lights at Walmart for $20. They are very thin, the lens is only 3/4" high and 4 inches wide or less - would tuck up under the front bumper on my '85SE nicely.<P>  Im wondering if wiring them in series would work, putting them at half voltage.<P>  they are intended to be high beam supplements - focused down the road in a pencil beam - which is what I want for visibility in the daytime - but I dont want them on full power in the daytime.<P>  Also thinking I could wire a switch to the dash to switch them onto full power (put them in parallel instead of series) to use them as real driving lights at night with the high beams. Could also use them to blink at other drivers during the day - thats one thing you cant do on a Fiero is blink your lights at someone, not without raising them first.<P>   Anyone know if you can run quartz halogen lamps at half voltage? I think thats how the running lights on my Saturn work - they are actually the high beams running on a lower voltage.<P>  Since most driving lights are grounded through the metal frame I could mount these to the bumper where its all plastic, and run my own ground wires. That would allow me to wire them in series for half brightness during the day, and then switch them to parallel for full intensity.||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||05-31-2001||02:34 PM||||Best thing to do is get one of the DRL modules from GM or the after market and wire the lamps to it. The Module will allow you to run the lamps full bright with the headlights on or as low output lamps durring the day. You should be able to hook the module up as normal, just replace the main lamps with the driving lamp.<P>If you get the GM one you want the one With Out "twilight sentinal."<P>GM will say the thing isn't for cars with popup headlights. Obiously because it can't open the lights... but that doesn't apply to you since you are connecting it to aftermarket lamps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||05-31-2001||03:31 PM||||hey thats cool - i was already contemplaing how i could wire them in series and parallel, and have them go off when the headlights are on, and go on full when the highbeams are on<P>  and go from half to full when you blink the hibeam switch at someone<P>   I was starting to think I would have a little relay village under the front hood.<P>  if the DRL (daytime running lights?!) module does most of this them maybe ill give up the ability to blink them on high during the day. Not a bad tradeoff if it saves me 4 hours of wiring.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||05-31-2001||03:33 PM||||now i wonder?! if you pull the highbeam level back on the turn signal with the headlights off, do the highbeams still flash under the hood?!<P>  this is like one of those refridgerator questions.<P>  it would be stupid if they do - but it would have taken additional effort by GM to change the wiring so they wont.<P>   gotta try it now.||||reg||1
